From 18fe1e167c2083f9dce8cc3d2ffce2cf46c22a6f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jesse Osiecki <jesse@jjo.ninja>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 18:20:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: cros_charge-control: Disable the sustainer
 before programming it

While the battery sustainer holds the state of charge inside its band,
the EC moves its own charge mode to IDLE or DISCHARGE. On such an EC a
SET command with mode NORMAL that arrives in that state makes the EC
drop the sustainer, even though the command carries bounds: the EC
returns to NORMAL with the sustainer off and charges to 100%.

That bites whenever userspace writes a threshold while the battery sits
at the limit: the write succeeds, sysfs keeps showing the threshold, and
the EC quietly charges past it. Seen with a boot-time service that
re-applies a stored limit, and with the Plasma settings page.

Reproduced from userspace on a Lenovo IdeaPad Duet 3 (google-trogdor
wormdingler, EC wormdingler_v2.0.6642): with the sustainer at 80/80 and
the EC in DISCHARGE, "SET NORMAL 80/80" leaves "NORMAL -1/-1";
"SET NORMAL -1/-1" followed by "SET NORMAL 80/80" leaves 80/80 and the
sustainer picks the mode up again a few seconds later. The EC console
prints "Sustain mode disabled" for the first case.

So when the sustainer is being programmed, send a SET that returns to
NORMAL with the sustainer off first, then the SET with the bounds, which
then changes no mode and is accepted.

Signed-off-by: Jesse Osiecki <jesse@jjo.ninja>
---
 drivers/power/supply/cros_charge-control.c | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/cros_charge-control.c b/drivers/power/supply/cros_charge-control.c
index a847db9f13..62af8cd368 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/cros_charge-control.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/cros_charge-control.c
@@ -96,6 +96,28 @@ static int cros_chctl_configure_ec(struct cros_chctl_priv *priv)
 		req.sustain_soc.upper = -1;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * While the sustainer holds the state of charge it switches the EC's
+	 * charge mode to IDLE or DISCHARGE by itself. A SET that moves the mode
+	 * back to NORMAL from there makes the EC drop the sustainer, even when
+	 * the SET carries bounds: the bounds are lost and charging resumes to
+	 * 100%. So when programming the sustainer, first return to NORMAL with
+	 * the sustainer off, then set the bounds in a second command, which no
+	 * longer changes the mode.
+	 */
+	if (req.mode == CHARGE_CONTROL_NORMAL && req.sustain_soc.lower >= 0) {
+		struct ec_params_charge_control off = {
+			.cmd = EC_CHARGE_CONTROL_CMD_SET,
+			.mode = CHARGE_CONTROL_NORMAL,
+			.sustain_soc = { .lower = -1, .upper = -1 },
+		};
+		int ret;
+
+		ret = cros_chctl_send_charge_control_cmd(priv->cros_ec, priv->cmd_version, &off);
+		if (ret < 0)
+			return ret;
+	}
+
 	return cros_chctl_send_charge_control_cmd(priv->cros_ec, priv->cmd_version, &req);
 }
 
